reviewer_concerns.value:
1. Concern (NAF4(w1); uatp; x5hb):
LLM judging can encode bias; taking the minimum score across judge ensemble may be overly conservative / unstable; 
The authors response: they did human evaluations validating judge reliability and explored different judge setups; 
But they don’t really resolve: (a) how sensitive results are to judge choice, (b) whether min aggregation is “correct” vs robust statistics, (c) the collusion/self-preference concern (x5hb follow-up explicitly says this is not answered: "Weaknesses of using LLM as a judge: The response does not fully address the risk of model collusion. Specifically, when the Red Team agent, Target agent, and Judge agent share the same base model (or family), there is a risk of bias where the Judge favors the Target's style or fails to recognize specific failure modes known to that model family. How does the framework mitigate this 'self-preference' or blind spot?")

2.  no convincing comparison vs standard (automated/manual) red teaming. this is raised by x5hb
Need quantitative comparison against a baseline attacker/red-teaming setup + cost/efficiency comparison.
It is not satisfied by the reviewer x5hb in the follow up discussion.

3. Generated scenarios could be fictional/hallucinatory; 
It is raised by uatp. Also mentioned in the follow up: "Third, on the Realism of Scenarios, I strongly recommend adding verification results to ensure scenario plausibility. Even with real APIs, LLMs suffer from hallucinations, and without strong constraints、seeds or relevance checks on the retrieved content, the model may still fabricate unrealistic or logically incoherent scenarios."
